#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb4ba432-2a8f-5769-8c7b-3bf2be07a4ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280908} {:answer/id #uuid "40bf93a9-0e1a-5d27-a8f0-f3f34430aa2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280909} {:answer/id #uuid "1453c279-fab3-529d-aaba-4443449a3b23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280910} {:answer/id #uuid "a0810cfc-2716-503e-8eda-9bd7b08acab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280911} {:answer/id #uuid "ce492c35-85b8-5d30-8cc7-1882be5a74c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce492c35-85b8-5d30-8cc7-1882be5a74c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280912} :answer-field/id #uuid "84ed8533-f1fb-5df4-95a9-e6288fd5e96f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4646eada-04b7-5167-b329-286673c2150b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38182" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $Q$ given below?\n\n- $Q$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- $Q$ is a singular matrix\n- $det(Q)=-5$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}|+6|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-3(8-14)+6(5-8) \\\\  & =0-3\\cdot (-6)+6\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =18-18 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(Q)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}]}
